# Package-manager cooldown: npm will not resolve a version published less than
# 14 days ago. ICTU recommendation 6, tracked in #119.
#
# Renovate already waits 14 days (minimumReleaseAge in renovate.json), but only
# for the updates it proposes itself. Lock-file maintenance hands the refresh to
# npm, and Renovate's documentation says its own cooldown cannot apply there, so
# this file is what covers the transitive tree. Renovate reads it, and for its
# own update pull requests uses whichever cutoff is stricter, its own or this.
# The same goes for `npm install` and `npm update` on a workstation.
#
# What it does not cover, measured on 19 September 2026:
# - `npm ci` ignores it on purpose and installs the lockfile as it is (npm/cli
#   #9281). CI only ever runs `npm ci`, so CI cannot fail on it.
# - npm older than 11.10 ignores it without a warning. Node 24.21.0, which
#   .nvmrc names, bundles npm 11.19.0 and honours this setting, so the caveat
#   no longer applies to anyone on the pinned version. scripts/check-deps.sh
#   still warns at every dev-server start and push, which now matters only on a
#   workstation running something older than .nvmrc.
#
#   This said "Node 22.23.2 ... bundles npm 10.9.8" until iou-architectuur#105:
#   it described the gap the Node 24 move in #80 had already closed.
#
# An urgent security fix may skip the cooldown, as the guideline allows: set the
# min-release-age flag to zero days on that one command line, never in this
# file, and say why in the pull request.
# Renovate's security pull requests do this themselves, retrying without the
# cutoff when npm answers ETARGET.
min-release-age=14
